The recipe applies cleanly to the two-argument toModuleName. Its relative addressing is used only by testModuleName, so the omission fails exactly one test. The original URI resolution was split into five hops:
- extension stripping;
- the relative or top-level decision;
- './' placement;
- a pass-through;
- the gold's segment walk.
The final conversion is still done by the intact one-argument overload.

The gold is the single owner because the trigger's target ('baz') is outside the requiring file's directory ('foo/'). No path without '..' reaches it from that file, so a one-line edit above the gold can only get the right answer in one of three ways, each recorded as a bypass:
- give the gold a different requiring file;
- compute the resolution itself;
- post-process the module name in a test-fitted way.

The gold's convention is pinned outside its own javadoc in three places. The facade's javadoc promises relative addressing with ./ and ../. PathUtil.collapseDots performs the same '..' step in production code and is reached via makeAbsolute from DepsGenerator. toModuleName(String) and its passing tests pin the conversion the gold hands its result to.

The correct configuration matches the original output on every testModuleName case and on top-level, './', single '..' and absolute-path inputs. It differs only on an interior '.' segment and on two or more '..' that climb above the top directory.

collectDefaults is a zero-argument, uniform fold over the enum: one predicate (enabledByDefault) and one OR accumulation. Its only omissions change every enabled feature or every disabled feature at once, so they fail 60-76 tests and break G5; the empty-accumulation case is also a T2 frozen-local trace. A fault that only one test catches would have to remove exactly one feature, which needs a step tied to that feature or position. The original computation has no such step, and neither do JsonFactory.Feature.collectDefaults, JsonParser.Feature.collectDefaults or any other production code, so it would have no external pin. Every form considered either leaves a checklist trace or fails no test:
- naming one feature is T3(a)/(d);
- seeding the fold from the first feature with the loop starting at begin + 1 leaves, after the omission, a loop that visibly skips the range start (T3(b)/T4);
- a run-commit or Horner-style fold has no effect on the real enum order or collapses every bit (T2);
- an ordinal-fits-in-int guard is never exercised.

Because the facade takes no input, a one-line OR of a constant mask at any hop would also restore a lost bit, so the gold could not be the unique owner. collectDefaults is the instance's only allowed target, so the instance is UNBUILDABLE.

